        ":topic/id": "8bdb85a0-8774-5298-bc46-db4f7969ae17",
        ":topic/knowledge-points": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184349,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186183945,
              ":question/id": "3e9586ee-12d1-5dbb-81f6-0f0a4998e00a",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-14402",
              ":question/problem": "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int x^2 e^{x^3+1}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Let's introduce the new variable $u = x^3+1.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm{d}u = x^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\cdot \\,{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "e803bd99-388f-5146-ab91-03fd7b658c2c",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Integrating Products Containing the Exponential Function"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184350,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186183946,
              ":question/id": "ab4772a2-f980-5ad7-a377-34e34b95be83",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-14403",
              ":question/problem": "Calculate  $\\displaystyle \\int x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Let's introduce the new variable $u= 1-x^4.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ \\textrm{d}u}{ \\textrm{d}x} = -4x^3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad x^3 \\, \\textrm{d}x = -\\dfrac{1}{4} \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int {x^3\\,{3^{1-x^4}} }\\, \\textrm{d}x \n&=\\int {{3^{1-x^4}}\\cdot x^3 }\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n&=  \\int {3^{u}} \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\textrm{d}u\\\\[5pt]\n                                                     &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\int {3^{u}}  \\textrm{d}u\\\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=  -\\dfrac{1}{4}\\cdot\\dfrac{3^{u}}{\\ln 3} + C\\\\[5pt]\n                                                     &=   -\\dfrac{3^{1-x^4}}{4\\ln 3}+ C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "e8720b44-317d-590e-a251-bfcec97e0513",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Integrating Products Containing Other Exponential Functions"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184351,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186183947,
              ":question/id": "70fd3433-e905-5643-8e55-0bdf774d781e",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-14404",
              ":question/problem": "Calculate  $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {e^{2x}} {e^{2x} + 1} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u = e^{2x}+1.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 2e^{2x}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\, \\textrm{d}u = e^{2x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|{e}^{2x}+1|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{2x}+1)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that $e^{2x} + 1 > 0$ for all $x.$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "ed3b6c3d-b840-50ef-9e6b-584bf14877b3",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "More Complex Cases"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186184352,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186183948,
              ":question/id": "a99e8d2c-1934-57a9-9a6a-578ec9230f6f",
